# City pedal-assist bike profile — Roue Libre
#
# Serves the main leg when the ride is made on a pedal-assist bike (SPEC.md
# §5, §6). It describes the same bike-share bike as `city-bike.brf` — same
# twenty-odd kilos of frame, same upright position, same narrow tyres, same
# absent gears — carrying a motor and a battery. Everything about the streets
# it may take, the surfaces it dislikes and the turns it pays for is therefore
# copied from that profile unchanged: the assistance changes how a hill and a
# distance are paid for, never where a bicycle is allowed to go.
#
# Three assignments, and only three, differ from `city-bike.brf`. Each is
# justified where it stands, and nothing else here should be read as a
# deliberate divergence.
#
# The cost factors multiply distance. A factor of 2 means a detour of up to
# twice the length will be accepted to avoid that kind of way.
#
# Licence: GPLv3, like the rest of the project.

---context:global

assign validForBikes = true

assign turnInstructionMode = 0

# --- what the motor changes ----------------------------------------------

# DIFFERS FROM city-bike.brf (1/3). The engine solves a constant-power
# equation for the speed of every section: the pedalling power against the
# rolling resistance, the slope and the air. The mechanical profile leaves it
# at the engine's default hundred watts, which is a rider pedalling steadily,
# and that is kept here — the rider of an assisted bike pedals no harder, that
# being the point of the assistance. What is added on top is the motor's.
#
# The figure is anchored on the one fact about a pedelec that is measured
# rather than felt: European law rates the motor at 250 W and **cuts the
# assistance off at 25 km/h**. Assistance tapers as that ceiling is neared, so
# a model that holds power constant must state the average contribution over a
# ride and not the motor's peak. Forty watts on top of the rider's hundred is
# the value that puts this profile's free-running speed on the flat at the
# cut-off itself — which is where a pedelec settles on an open flat street,
# and the reason its ceiling is a figure riders recognise. Above it the motor
# gives nothing and gravity still gives what it gives, which is why no ceiling
# is written here: on a descent this bike coasts like any other, and capping it
# at 25 would have made the assisted bike the slower one downhill.
assign bikerPower = 140

# DIFFERS FROM city-bike.brf (2/3). Motor and battery weigh some five kilos
# more than the bare share bike, and mass is what a climb is paid in. The
# mechanical profile leaves the engine's ninety kilos — a seventy-kilo rider on
# a twenty-kilo bike — so the assisted one carries ninety-five. It is stated
# rather than ignored because it is the one term where the assistance costs
# something: on a five per cent grade those five kilos take about four per cent
# off the speed the motor buys. Small beside what the motor gives, and on the
# honest side of the account.
assign totalMass = 95

# DIFFERS FROM city-bike.brf (3/3). The Lille metropolis is flat — some fifty
# metres of climb in all — and the mechanical profile still pays forty metres
# of detour per metre climbed, because a heavy share bike climbs poorly and its
# rider knows it. An assisted one does not: the motor gives most on precisely
# the sections where it is asked for, so **a hill stops being a reason to go
# round**. Lowered to a quarter of the mechanical figure rather than to zero:
# a climb still takes longer than the flat, which the kinematic model above
# already charges in minutes, and a bridge ramp taken for nothing remains worth
# a short detour. The cutoff is untouched: below two per cent, neither bike is
# climbing anything.
assign downhillcost = 0
assign downhillcutoff = 2.0
assign uphillcost = 10
assign uphillcutoff = 2.0

---context:way

# Every change of direction costs: slowing down, looking, and getting a heavy
# bike moving again. Enough to prefer a simple route at equal length, not
# enough to lengthen the journey appreciably. An assisted bike is heavier still
# and pulls away no more willingly at walking pace, the assistance being
# proportional to what the rider puts in: unchanged.
assign turncost = 60

# Cost of entering a way: discourages chains of short, mismatched sections.
assign initialcost = 0

# --- wrong ways ----------------------------------------------------------

# Contraflow cycling is very common in Lille's 30 km/h zones: refusing it
# would rule out half the city centre.
assign bicycle_may_go_against =
  or oneway:bicycle=no
  or cycleway=opposite|opposite_lane|opposite_track
  highway=living_street|pedestrian

assign is_wrong_way =
  and reversedirection=yes
  and oneway=yes|true|1
  not bicycle_may_go_against

# --- access --------------------------------------------------------------

assign bicycle_refused = bicycle=no|private|use_sidepath

assign closed_to_all =
  and access=no|private not bicycle=yes|designated|permissive

# Fast roads: forbidden to bikes, and deadly if one strays onto them.
assign motorway_like = highway=motorway|motorway_link|trunk|trunk_link

assign is_forbidden =
  or is_wrong_way or bicycle_refused or closed_to_all motorway_like

# --- surface -------------------------------------------------------------

# Cobbles are everywhere in the North, and they are the real comfort question
# for a bike with no suspension and narrow tyres. Good-quality setts stay
# rideable; old cobbles shake enough that one would clearly rather go round.
# The motor drives the wheel, not the tyres or the springs the bike does not
# have, so these are the mechanical profile's figures exactly.
assign surface_penalty =
  switch surface=asphalt|paved|concrete|paving_stones|metal|wood 1.0
  switch surface=sett 1.5
  switch surface=cobblestone 2.0
  switch surface=compacted|fine_gravel 1.3
  switch surface=gravel|ground|dirt|earth|grass|sand|mud 2.4
  # Surface not stated: in town, it is asphalt nine times out of ten.
  1.0

# --- kind of way ---------------------------------------------------------

# Infrastructure built for cycling.
assign cycle_way =
  or highway=cycleway
  or cyclestreet=yes
  or bicycle_road=yes
  and highway=path|footway bicycle=designated

# Calmed streets where a bike mixes well with cars.
assign calm_way = highway=living_street|residential|service|unclassified|road

assign base_cost

  switch is_forbidden 100000

  # Steps are taken with the bike in hand. It is possible, never desirable,
  # and an assisted share bike is heavier still.
  switch highway=steps 40

  # A pedestrian area is crossed at walking pace. Tolerated, never preferred.
  switch highway=pedestrian 3.0

  # A pavement with no cycling permission: passable on foot, to be avoided.
  switch and highway=footway not bicycle=designated|yes 8.0

  switch cycle_way 1.0
  switch calm_way 1.2
  switch highway=track 1.8

  switch highway=tertiary|tertiary_link 1.5
  switch highway=secondary|secondary_link 2.1
  switch highway=primary|primary_link 3.0

  2.5

# The final cost combines the kind of way with its surface: a cobbled cycle
# track is not a smooth cycle track.
assign costfactor
  switch equal base_cost 100000 100000
  multiply base_cost surface_penalty

---context:node

# What forces one off the bike, or forbids it outright.
assign initialcost =
  switch bicycle=no|private 1000000
  # A barrier to get round costs the time of putting a foot down.
  switch barrier=gate|lift_gate|swing_gate|cycle_barrier|block|bollard 400
  # Traffic lights cost an average wait, which justifies a slight detour by a
  # route that has fewer of them.
  switch highway=traffic_signals 120
  0
